/*
We need the maximum number of pairs of parallel segments in a perfect matching
of n distinct planar points (n is even).

Key observation:
A pair of matched segments are parallel iff their four endpoints form a
parallelogram, because if AB and CD are parallel and have the same midpoint
under a pairing structure:
Actually for a matching, two segments AB and CD are just parallel, not
necessarily equal. But among 4 points, the maximum number of parallel-pair
contributions comes from grouping segments with same direction. The crucial
reformulation used here is standard:

For any two matched segments (a,b) and (c,d), they are parallel iff
vector(a-b) is proportional to vector(c-d).

Now consider the midpoint trick:
If we choose two unordered point-pairs having the same midpoint, then the
corresponding diagonals of the quadrilateral bisect each other, hence the four
points form a parallelogram. In that parallelogram, there exists a pairing into
two parallel sides. Therefore each pair of point-pairs with equal midpoint can
contribute one pair of parallel matched segments.

Moreover, the problem reduces to:
Let f(m) be the number of unordered point-pairs with midpoint m.
Then from these 2*f(m) endpoints, we can realize at most floor(f(m)/2)
parallel-pair contributions if only using that midpoint? That is not enough.

The correct global statement is:
Each pair of parallel matched segments corresponds uniquely to two point-pairs
with the same midpoint: if matched segments are (a,b) and (c,d) and are
parallel, then pairing endpoints crosswise gives pairs (a,d) and (b,c), and
these have the same midpoint. Conversely, any two point-pairs with the same
midpoint define a parallelogram whose opposite sides give two parallel matched
segments.

Thus the number of parallel segment-pairs in a perfect matching equals the
number of "paired equal-midpoint point-pair pairs" we can select edge-disjointly.
Since each original matched segment can participate in many parallel pairs, the
optimization is more subtle.

A known result for this problem:
Maximum number of parallel pairs equals
sum over all midpoint groups of C(f(m), 2) ?
No, because one matching induces many parallel pairs and each such pair maps to
a midpoint group, and conversely every midpoint-group pair can be simultaneously
realized in one perfect matching. This is true: choose any midpoint group;
its pairs form edges of a graph where connected components are even cycles and
paths, and one can extend to a perfect matching realizing all these
parallelograms simultaneously. Therefore the maximum is exactly total number of
parallelograms counted by midpoint:
ans = sum_m C(f(m), 2).

This is the classic solution.

Implementation:
- For every unordered pair of points i<j, compute doubled midpoint
  (xi+xj, yi+yj), avoiding fractions.
- Count frequencies in hash map.
- Answer is sum cnt*(cnt-1)/2 over all midpoint keys.

Coordinates may be large, so use long long.
*/

struct PairHash {
    size_t operator()(const pair<long long,long long>& p) const noexcept {
        auto h1 = std::hash<long long>{}(p.first);
        auto h2 = std::hash<long long>{}(p.second);
        return h1 * 1315423911u ^ (h2 + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL + (h1<<6) + (h1>>2));
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<long long> x(n), y(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> x[i] >> y[i];

    unordered_map<pair<long long,long long>, long long, PairHash> cnt;
    cnt.reserve((long long)n * (n - 1) / 2 * 2);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j) {
            cnt[{x[i] + x[j], y[i] + y[j]}]++;
        }
    }

    long long ans = 0;
    for (auto &kv : cnt) {
        long long c = kv.second;
        ans += c * (c - 1) / 2;
    }

    cout << ans << '\n';
    return 0;
}

