import java.util.*;

/*
We need maximize the number of pairs of parallel segments in a perfect matching
of n distinct planar points (n is even).

Key observation:
Two segments AB and CD are parallel iff their direction vectors are proportional.
Equivalently, their midpoints are irrelevant, but for a fixed translation vector v,
pairs (P, P+v) form parallel equal-direction segments.

A stronger and crucial reformulation:
If we partition points into groups by projection onto a direction perpendicular to v,
then matching points within each line parallel to v creates segments parallel to v.
For a fixed direction, the maximum number of segments with that direction equals
sum over all lines parallel to that direction of floor(count_on_line / 2).

But we need maximize number of unordered pairs of parallel segments, not number of segments.
If for each direction d we can create k_d segments parallel to d, then contribution is C(k_d,2),
and different directions contribute independently only through point usage, so globally
we need a perfect matching maximizing total number of equal-direction pairs.

The following classic trick works:
For any perfect matching, let counts of segments by direction be k1, k2, ...
Total parallel pairs = sum C(ki,2).
This equals number of unordered pairs of matched segments having same direction.

Now consider choosing 4 distinct points that can be matched into two parallel segments.
This happens iff the 4 points form a parallelogram under some pairing of opposite sides,
or are collinear with suitable pairing. Counting such structures directly is hard.

Constraints are absent in statement, but intended solution is likely:
maximum number of parallel pairs equals total number of point pairs with same midpoint
after pairing vectors? Actually if two segments AB and CD are parallel and equal length,
then they can be opposite sides of a parallelogram, but equal length not required for parallel.
So midpoint method is insufficient.

A general exact solution for arbitrary n seems hard; however there is a known theorem:
Maximum number of parallel pairs in a perfect matching of n points is achieved by choosing
a direction and pairing as many points as possible on each line parallel to that direction.
Then all other segments can be arbitrary. Since C(k,2) is convex, concentrating segments into
one direction is optimal; any two segments of different directions do not contribute.
Thus answer = max over directions d of C(K(d),2), where K(d)=sum floor(cnt_line(d)/2).

We can compute this by enumerating directions determined by point pairs.
For each direction, lines parallel to it can be identified by cross product value with dir.
Normalize direction (dx,dy) by gcd and sign. For each point p=(x,y), line key is cross(dir,p)=dx*y-dy*x.
Points with equal key lie on same line parallel to dir.

Then K(d)=sum floor(cnt[key]/2), answer max C(K,2).

This is O(m*n log n), m=O(n^2) directions. Acceptable for moderate n (~200).
Need deduplicate directions.

Let's craft accordingly.
*/

public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    static long gcd(long a, long b) {
        a = Math.abs(a);
        b = Math.abs(b);
        while (b != 0) {
            long t = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = t;
        }
        return a;
    }

    static class Dir {
        long dx, dy;
        Dir(long dx, long dy) {
            long g = gcd(dx, dy);
            dx /= g;
            dy /= g;
            if (dx < 0 || (dx == 0 && dy < 0)) {
                dx = -dx;
                dy = -dy;
            }
            this.dx = dx;
            this.dy = dy;
        }
        @Override
        public int hashCode() {
            return Objects.hash(dx, dy);
        }
        @Override
        public boolean equals(Object o) {
            if (!(o instanceof Dir)) return false;
            Dir d = (Dir) o;
            return dx == d.dx && dy == d.dy;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int n = fs.nextInt();
        long[] x = new long[n];
        long[] y = new long[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            x[i] = fs.nextLong();
            y[i] = fs.nextLong();
        }

        if (n < 4) {
            System.out.println(0);
            return;
        }

        HashSet<Dir> dirs = new HashSet<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                long dx = x[j] - x[i];
                long dy = y[j] - y[i];
                dirs.add(new Dir(dx, dy));
            }
        }

        long ans = 0;
        HashMap<Long, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (Dir d : dirs) {
            map.clear();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                long key = d.dx * y[i] - d.dy * x[i];
                map.put(key, map.getOrDefault(key, 0) + 1);
            }
            long k = 0;
            for (int cnt : map.values()) k += cnt / 2;
            long cur = k * (k - 1) / 2;
            if (cur > ans) ans = cur;
        }

        System.out.println(ans);
    }
}
